# Normalise line endings for everything this repository authors.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Every architecture package is an IMMUTABLE INPUT. `v1.3.1` must round-trip
# byte-identically against _input/acos-phase2-architecture-v1.3.1.zip, so git performs no
# line-ending conversion on it in either direction. A checkout on a machine with
# core.autocrlf=true would otherwise rewrite every file in it and break that property.
#
# `v1.3.2` (phase2-v1.3.2-errata.md, JCS-01) and `v1.3.3` (phase2-v1.3.3-errata.md, APF-01 /
# MLT-01 / FHT-01 / SWR-01) are repository-authored package issues rather than extracted
# zips, so neither has a round-trip target of its own. Both are held under the same rule so
# that a diff against the previous issue shows the normative delta and nothing else; their
# files are committed LF, like v1.3.1's.
docs/architecture/** -text

# The deployed control-artifact bytes. `50 §3c`: `content_hash = SHA-256(EXACT_ARTIFACT_
# BYTES)`, and "Any byte difference — including a line terminator, a trailing newline or a
# byte-order mark — changes the digest and therefore changes the artifact's identity."
# `50 §2b` says the same of the class-20 specification specifically: "LF (`0x0A`) line
# terminators exclusively; the file contains no `0x0D` byte. A CRLF copy is a different
# artifact and fails verification."
#
# `eol=lf` above would already produce LF on checkout. `-text` is stronger and is what this
# directory needs: git performs NO conversion in either direction, so a clone on a machine
# with `core.autocrlf=true` cannot move a content hash and cannot invalidate an owner
# signature by rewriting a file nobody edited.
artifacts/control/** -text

_input/*.zip binary
package-lock.json -diff
